<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The parties married in 2019. The husband&rsquo;s petition alleged that the wife quarrelled with his mother, left the matrimonial home within a month, and had been diagnosed with schizophrenia, bipolar disorder and a seizure disorder, which she refused to treat; village panchayats were said to have failed. He sued for divorce before the Family Court, Koderma. The wife did not appear, and in January 2026 the Family Court granted an ex parte decree of divorce on the testimony of three witnesses, including the husband himself — without any medical records of substance and without examining any treating doctor.</p>
<p>In appeal, the wife alleged dowry demands, assault, ouster from the matrimonial home and the taking of her stridhan, and pointed to a pending maintenance case at Dhanbad.</p>

<h2>A medical label cannot carry a decree</h2>
<p>The Division Bench&rsquo;s analysis begins with <em>Ram Narain Gupta v. Rameshwari Gupta</em>: a psychiatric diagnosis, even if established, does not by itself dissolve a marriage. The statutory ground under Section 13(1)(iii) demands a disorder of a kind and degree making cohabitation unreasonable, and the decree under challenge had in substance treated the illness narrative as cruelty under Section 13(1)(ia). The entire foundation was the husband&rsquo;s say-so about diagnosis and medication refusal.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment:</strong> &ldquo;Mental illness cannot, by itself, be made a ground for stigmatizing a spouse or dissolving the marriage.&rdquo; The court added that &ldquo;the absence of any cogent medical evidence assumes significance in the facts of the present case&rdquo;.</p>
</div>

<h2>Ex parte does not mean automatic</h2>
<p>The second — and for daily practice, the more important — strand of the judgment concerns the Family Court&rsquo;s duty when the respondent is absent. An ex parte proceeding relieves the petitioner of cross-examination, not of proof. The ground pleaded must still be strictly established; the court must still test the evidence against the statute; and a decree that rests on an evidentiary vacuum is perverse. On that reasoning the Division Bench quashed the decree.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For petitioners.</strong> If mental disorder or illness-related cruelty is the case, build the medical record: treatment papers properly proved, and the treating psychiatrist examined. A petition that pleads diagnosis but proves none will not survive appeal, however smoothly it proceeded ex parte.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For absent respondents.</strong> A first appeal under Section 19 of the Family Courts Act remains available against an ex parte decree, alongside the remedy of seeking recall before the Family Court itself. Appellate courts examine whether the ground was actually proved, not merely whether the procedure was followed.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For Delhi practice.</strong> The same discipline binds Delhi&rsquo;s Family Courts: strict proof of the matrimonial ground, with the High Court of Delhi exercising the corresponding appellate scrutiny under Section 19.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>Allegations of psychiatric illness appear in a significant share of contested divorces, and they carry a stigmatising force that outlasts the litigation. The Supreme Court&rsquo;s jurisprudence — from <em>Ram Narain Gupta</em> onward — has consistently required courts to look past labels to degree, consequence and proof. This judgment applies that discipline at the point where it is most often relaxed: the undefended case. For the practitioner, the lesson is symmetrical. The petitioner who treats an absent respondent as a shortcut invites reversal; the respondent who ignores summons forfeits the trial but not, ultimately, the law&rsquo;s insistence on proof.</p>
